<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="PowerPC:BE:32:default">
<!--
    kuna option bejoin callers (the default), GH-904: a big-endian long long
    the callee alone cannot prove joins high word first when a caller reads
    its second register after the call.

    PowerPC returns a long long in r3:r4 with the HIGH word in r3.  A 64-bit
    sum compiles to the bytes of the int that returns its high word:
        add64:  addc 4,4,6 / adde 3,3,5 / blr       return a + b;
        add_hi: addc 4,4,6 / adde 3,3,5 / blr       return (a + b) >> 32;
    so with option bejoin evidence add64 printed its halves swapped:
        return CONCAT44(a1 + a3,a0 + a2 + (uint4)CARRY4(a1,a3));   (bug)
    and its callers read the low word as the high one.  A caller compiled
    against int add_hi() never reads r4 after the call, which the call
    clobbered; c_add stores r4 (stw 4,4(9)), so add64 returns both words.
    pass returns add64's pair through its frame (no tail call), and c_pass
    stores both words of it.  keep never writes r4, so a caller may keep its
    own value there across the call (gcc's IPA-RA), and a read of r4 after
    keep says nothing about keep.

    Stage: P4 calls and prototypes (output-prototype), option bejoin.
    Pass 1 is the default (callers), pass 2 option bejoin evidence.

    Image: decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/bejoin_callers_ppc32_O2
    (bejoin_callers.c, powerpc-linux-gnu-gcc 13.3 -O2 -nostdlib -static).

    Assert #1: the default returns add64's sum high word first; evidence
      swaps it, and add_hi keeps the old join in both passes.
    Assert #2: the default reads add64's and pass's pair r3 high in c_add and
      c_pass and stores the high word at the lower address; evidence reads
      them r3 low.
    Assert #3: keep and c_hi stay as they were in both passes.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">4e80002000000000</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
